    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a soup with real meat, real greens and real pasta in it. The ground turkey is most of the ticket. Everything else in that pot is pocket change.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eThe ground turkey is the entire budget.\u003c/strong\u003e Everything else here is broth, vegetables and pasta. Buy the turkey when the price drops, then freeze it flat in one pound sheets so it thaws in an hour instead of a day.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFrozen chopped spinach beats fresh for this pot.\u003c/strong\u003e It costs less per usable ounce, it keeps for months, and it goes into hot broth straight from the box. Nobody is washing sand out of anything.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy parmesan in a tub, not a shaker.\u003c/strong\u003e The shaker stuff costs more per ounce and it will not melt into the broth the same way. Grate it yourself and you get a better soup for less money.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFresh basil is the one splurge here.\u003c/strong\u003e If the packs at the store look tired, leave it out. The soup holds up fine without it and you keep the money."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Mix the ground turkey with the bread crumbs, eggs, milk, some of the grated parmesan and the chopped fresh basil. Season with salt and pepper, then roll the mix into small meatballs about the size of a grape.",
                    "Line a sheet pan, spread the meatballs out, and bake at 400F until browned, roughly 15 minutes. They finish cooking in the broth later, so do not fuss over the centers.",
                    "Chop the onion, carrots and celery small and sweat them in olive oil in your big pot over medium heat until soft, about 8 minutes.",
                    "Pour in the chicken broth, bring it to a simmer, then add the pasta shells and cook until they are just shy of tender.",
                    "Stir in the kale and the thawed chopped spinach and simmer until the greens go dark and silky.",
                    "Slide the meatballs into the pot, simmer a few minutes more so the flavors marry, then kill the heat and stir in the rest of the parmesan."
                ],
